#!/usr/bin/env bash
# kit-audit - lists the forge's attestations, alerts, controls, and waivers, and the policy's rules (kit verb: workflow)
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# kit audit [--text | --json | --jq]
#
# Draws what the forge and the policy say of what the repository can be trusted for, a stage for each of Trust's tabs
# in kittui. It changes nothing. The stages, in order:
#
#   evidence → findings → controls → waivers → policy
#
# kit --workspace=<workspace> audit, the kit option before the verb, draws each stage across a workspace's members
# with checkouts, as kit workspace does: a group's name, or a repository's host and path, as kit workspaces names it;
# none names no workspace. Without it, the workspace is the repository here, and outside one, the last group used, as
# kit workspaces --current says. On a terminal, kit audit opens Trust in kittui, and kit audit <stage> opens it on
# that tab, maximized. Without one, or with --text, it prints the stages as text; with --json or --jq, as JSON.
#
#   --text              prints the stages as text, even on a terminal
#   --json              the stages as one JSON object: stages, then each stage's document under its name, null for one
#                       that could not be read
#   --jq                the same JSON, formatted by jq for a person
#   --stages            prints the stages, one per line, in order, the same over any workspace
#   --marks             prints a mark per stage, as one JSON object: evidence's moves with the forge's runs and
#                       releases, findings' with its runs and alerts, each scanner's off or refused where it is,
#                       policy's with kit.yml and .kit/policy; controls and waivers have none, null, as each is read
#                       again only on Refresh, kittui offering no Watch for them
#   evidence            the attestations of the 5 newest releases' assets and the 20 newest artifacts
#   findings            the code scanning, Dependabot, and secret scanning alerts, open and dismissed
#   controls            the rulesets and the protection that guard the default branch
#   waivers             who may bypass each control, and each alert dismissed
#   policy              each rule kit policy judges by, as kit policy --rules lists them
#
# kit audit --stages
#
# kit audit --marks
#
# kit audit evidence [--text | --json | --jq]
#
# kit audit findings [--text | --json | --jq]
#
# kit audit controls [--text | --json | --jq]
#
# kit audit waivers [--text | --json | --jq]
#
# kit audit policy [--text | --json | --jq]
#
# With --json, a stage prints {rows, errors}, and limited where the forge holds more than it read. A row is {key, …,
# links}: key is the row's own in its stage, and links gives where a value leads, by its field. Every row says where it
# is from: organization and repository, the owner and the name of the repository kit read it in, as its primary remote's
# URL names them past the host, no organization for a primary on this machine; and source, the forge, as github or
# gitlab. policy's rows say where each rule is in force, as policy says below. Over a workspace, each row gains member, its host and path,
# and a member that could not be read is {member, error} under errors, while every other member still draws. A
# repository with no forge, or a forge without the stage's feature, gives no rows and no errors; a feature the forge has
# off for this repository, as secret scanning disabled, gives no rows and {feature, off: true} under errors, which is no
# failure.
#
#   evidence            {subject, digest, kind, signer, age}, keyed by the digest and the kind: subject the asset's
#                       or artifact's name; kind the statement's predicate type, null where GitHub keeps the bundle
#                       apart, compressed, which kit does not read; signer the workflow that signed it,
#                       <owner>/<repo>/<path>@<ref>, github for one GitHub made itself, or null; age in seconds. The
#                       subject links to the repository's attestations. GitHub reads a digest's attestations a
#                       request each, 50 digests at most; past 5 releases, 20 artifacts, or 50 digests, the document
#                       is limited. GitLab and Azure DevOps keep none kit reads
#   findings            {tool, number, rule, severity, state, path, age}, keyed by the tool and the number: tool
#                       code-scanning, dependabot, or secret-scanning; rule what found it, a rule's id, an advisory's,
#                       or a secret's type; severity in lower case, null for a secret; state open or dismissed; path
#                       where it was found. The rule links to the alert's page. GitHub reads each scanner's every
#                       page; a refusal names the scope to add, gh auth refresh --scopes security_events, or who can
#                       grant the access. GitLab reads its vulnerability report, SAST for code scanning, dependency
#                       scanning for Dependabot, and secret detection, off on a tier without it; GitLab leaves out a
#                       vulnerability the caller may not see, so the tab reads empty, never refused. Azure DevOps
#                       keeps none kit reads
#   controls            {id, control, target, enforcement, rules, kind}, keyed by the id: control its name;
#                       target the refs it names; enforcement active, evaluate, or disabled; rules its kinds of rule,
#                       null where the forge shows them only to an admin; kind ruleset or protection. The control
#                       links to its page. GitHub reads its rulesets, its own and its organization's, in one GraphQL
#                       request, and the default branch's protection; GitLab its protected branches and push rules;
#                       Azure DevOps the default branch's policies
#   waivers             {waives, actor, kind, reason, by, until, hidden}, keyed by what it waives and the actor:
#                       waives a control's id, or an alert as <tool> <number>, each linking that row; actor who may
#                       bypass; kind bypass or dismissal; reason the bypass's mode, or the dismissal's reason and
#                       comment; by who dismissed it; until when it ends, null for never; hidden, on the one bypass
#                       row of a control's bypassers the forge hides or kit cannot name, keyed <id> hidden, how many,
#                       its actor null, else null. The reason links to its page. The bypasses are the forge's own,
#                       GitHub's rulesets' bypass actors and its protection's; the dismissals are the alerts findings
#                       reads, each scanner read once for both, and a scanner off here, or one that refuses the caller,
#                       is under errors as in findings while the rest still draw. GitLab's are its dismissed
#                       vulnerabilities; Azure DevOps keeps none kit reads
#   policy              {source, organization, repository, branch, scope, rule, pattern, from, engine}, keyed by the
#                       rule and where it is in force: a row per rule kit policy --rules --json prints in each checkout
#                       of the clone with a branch, source the primary's forge, and the rows that agree merged, each at
#                       the narrowest place that holds them, scope naming it: branch, repository, organization, source,
#                       or workspace, the finer fields null; a place the clone has none of, as the organization of
#                       one with no remote, is ""
#
# Exits 0 when the stages are drawn, 1 when a stage could not be read, after what was, and 2 on a usage error, or
# outside a repository's working tree with no workspace.
#
# see: modules/bash/apps.sh, modules/bash/apps/audit.sh, modules/jq/apps.jq, docs/apps.md

set -o errexit -o nounset -o pipefail
# shellcheck source=modules/bash/1st.sh
source "$(dirname "$(readlink -f "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")")/../modules/bash/1st.sh"
kit:enter
use apps
workspaces:misplaced audit "$@" || exit $?
guard:arguments audit "$@" || exit $?
apps:main audit "$@"

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
